grad - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":"The course introduces students to the conceptual foundations and practical uses of transformer-based language models, their structure, capabilities, and limitations. Students will learn to interact with both local open-weight models and remote API-based systems. Through a sequence of hands-on projects, students design and build custom AI tools and applications such as personal interactive chatbot and recommendation system, Q&A document assistants (\"talk to my documents\"), LLM-based debate agents, and personal tutoring or research agents. Emphasis is placed on experimentation, system design, validation, and responsible use of LLMs. The course is for students with basic programming experience. Prerequisite: CS 101, CS 105, CS 107, CS 124."},{"crn":"74750","code":"G4","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":660,"end":740,"location":"138 Henry Administration Bldg","instructors":["Kim, K"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":"Advanced engineering analysis of fuel cell technologies, focusing on PEMFC, DBFC, MCFC, and SOFC systems. Covers microscopic electrochemical mecahnisms, multi-phase transport phenomena, and device-level degradation. Integrates state-of-the-art rsearch through peer-reviewed literature to explore stack design, thermal management, and system integration for stationary and mobile application. Graduate-lvel section emphasizes critical synthesis of recent advancements and independent research evaluation through specialized academic presentations. Prerequisites: Jr, Sr, or graduate standing. Basic understanding of thermodynamic principles. CHEM 102, PHYS 212, MATH 285, ME 200 (or CHBE 321, MSE 401, PHYS 213) or equivalent recommended. NPRE or ES 470 recommended. Or, consent of instructor."},{"crn":"74749","code":"UG3","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":660,"end":740,"location":"138 Henry Administration Bldg","instructors":["Kim, K"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":"Advanced engineering analysis of fuel cell technologies, focusing on PEMFC, DBFC, MCFC, and SOFC systems. Covers microscopic electrochemical mechanisms, multi-phase transport phenomena, and device-level degradation. Integrates state-of-the-art research through peer-reviewed literature to explore stack design, thermal management, and system integration for stationary and mobile applications. Prerequisites: Jr, Sr, or graduate standing. Basic understanding of thermodynamic principles. CHEM 102, PHYS 212, MATH 285, ME 200 (or CHBE 321, MSE 401, PHYS 213) or equivalent recommended. NPRE or ES 470 recommended. Or, consent of instructor."}]},{"id":"NPRE 521","title":"Interact of Radiation w/Matter","creditHours":"4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"34799","code":"A","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":900,"end":1010,"location":"1047 Sidney Lu Mech Engr Bldg","instructors":["Xi, J"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"NPRE 526","title":"Plasma-Material Interactions","creditHours":"4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"72305","code":"PMI","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"1302 Siebel Center for Comp Sci","instructors":["Andruczyk, D"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"NPRE 531","title":"Nuclear Materials","creditHours":"4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"39059","code":"D","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":660,"end":740,"location":"2036 Campus Instructional Facility","instructors":["Vergari, L"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"NPRE 554","title":"Independent Lab Investigations","creditHours":"1 TO 8 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"10072","code":"","type":"Independent